  "description": "An NE555 timer flashes a red LED about once a second from a 9 V battery, laid out the way the Learn guides suggest: https://perfboarddesigner.com/learn/\n\n**Power in.** The battery's leads go into the screw terminal J1 on the left edge, + at the top. D2 (1N4001) protects the circuit from a battery connected the wrong way round, and C3 (100 µF) steadies the supply right where it comes in.\n\n**Rails.** Bare tinned wire on the solder side carries +9 V along the top and ground along the bottom, and a ground bus runs from J1 past C3 to U1.1.\n\n**The timer.** U1 sits in the middle, in an 8-pin socket, with C4 (100 nF) right across its supply pins U1.8 and U1.1. The red jumper ties U1.4 (reset) to +9 V. A short link under the socket, on the solder side, joins U1.2 to U1.6.\n\n**Timing.** R1 (1 kΩ), R2 (68 kΩ) and C1 (10 µF) set the speed: about 0.48 s on and 0.47 s off. C2 (10 nF) keeps U1.5 quiet.\n\n**Output.** U1.3 lights D1 through R3 (470 Ω), at about 10 mA.\n\n**Mounting.** The four corners are kept clear for M3 screws.",
